            stimulus: ['The Theory of Opposites', 'The Christian Church'],
            model:
              "One primary reason Galen’s ideas continued to be believed was that his medical theories seemed logical and offered practical treatments. Galen expanded Hippocrates’ Four Humours into the Theory of Opposites. This gave doctors a clear system for treating illness: if a patient had a cold, wet fever, they were given hot, dry remedies like pepper or mustard. Because this gave doctors and ordinary people an understandable framework for illness, it provided reassuring explanations even when treatments failed to cure deadly epidemics. Therefore, Galen’s ideas continued to be believed because they provided a practical, common-sense explanation for illness that medieval people could easily understand.<br><br>Furthermore, Galen’s writings were actively supported and protected by the medieval Catholic Church. Galen believed that every part of the human body was purposefully created by a single God, which fit perfectly with Christian teachings. Because the Church controlled universities and book-copying, it declared Galen’s texts infallible and banned criticism of his work. In medical lectures, professors read aloud from Galen while an assistant cut open a body; if the corpse did not match Galen's drawings, professors claimed the body was deformed rather than admitting Galen was wrong. Therefore, Galen's ideas continued to be believed because the Church made it impossible for medical students to challenge his authority.<br><br>Finally, there was a total lack of alternative scientific tools or human dissection to disprove Galen's errors. Human dissection was heavily restricted by the Church, and doctors had no microscopes to see bacteria or physiological instruments to test blood flow. Because Galen had dissected animals like pigs and apes rather than humans, his mistakes remained undiscovered for centuries. Without modern scientific equipment or the freedom to experiment on human bodies, medieval doctors had no way of testing whether Galen’s theories were correct. Therefore, Galen’s ideas continued to be believed because doctors had neither the tools nor the scientific evidence to prove his theories wrong.",
