            model:
              "One fundamental reason medieval hospitals provided ‘care not cure’ was that they were religious institutions rather than medical facilities. Hospitals were run by monks and nuns and paid for by the Catholic Church or wealthy lords seeking forgiveness for their sins. The Latin word 'hospitium' meant hospitality, meaning hospitals were built as shelters for the elderly, poor, and pilgrims rather than places to treat the sick. Wards were arranged so that patient beds faced directly toward the chapel altar, ensuring patients could watch mass, confess their sins, and prepare their souls for heaven. Therefore, hospitals provided 'care not cure' because their primary purpose was saving the patient's soul through prayer rather than curing their physical body.<br><br>Furthermore, medieval hospitals deliberately lacked medically trained staff and surgical equipment. Monks and nuns had no medical training; their duties consisted of basic nursing care, such as washing patients, providing clean linens, and feeding them warm soup. Hospitals could not afford university-trained physicians, who charged high fees and only treated wealthy clients. Moreover, hospitals turned away people suffering from contagious diseases like leprosy and the plague, meaning they rarely admitted patients who needed active medical treatment. Therefore, hospitals provided 'care not cure' because they did not have the doctors, medicines, or equipment needed to cure illnesses.<br><br>Finally, medieval Christian doctrine taught that physical illness was sent directly by God to cleanse human sins. Priests and monks believed that attempting to cure a disease medically might interfere with God’s divine will, whereas prayer and repentance would heal the patient spiritually. As a result, hospital staff saw their Christian duty as offering compassionate comfort and shelter while letting God decide whether the patient lived or died. Therefore, hospitals provided 'care not cure' because religious belief placed spiritual salvation far above physical medicine.",
