              "One major reason for changes in medical communication was the invention of the movable-type printing press. Invented by Johannes Gutenberg in c.1440 and brought to England by William Caxton in 1476, the printing press allowed thousands of identical books to be produced quickly and cheaply. Prior to this, books were hand-copied by monks in monasteries, which was slow, expensive, and subject to Church censorship. Crucially, the printing press allowed new anatomical discoveries, such as Andreas Vesalius’s De Fabrica (1543), to be printed with exact anatomical drawings and shared simultaneously across European universities without errors. Therefore, medical communication changed because the printing press bypassed Church control and allowed accurate scientific discoveries to spread rapidly across Europe.<br><br>Furthermore, communication changed fundamentally due to the founding of the Royal Society in London in 1660. Granted a Royal Charter by King Charles II in 1662, the Royal Society promoted scientific experimentation under the motto 'Nullius in Verba' ('Take nobody's word for it'). In 1665, they began publishing Philosophical Transactions, Europe’s first peer-reviewed scientific journal. This created an international network where scientists could publish experimental results, critique each other's work, and read about new microscopic discoveries like Robert Hooke’s Micrographia (1665). Therefore, communication changed because the Royal Society created an official platform for scientists to share, debate, and verify new discoveries together.<br><br>Finally, communication changed because the Renaissance and Humanism weakened the authority of the Catholic Church. Following the Protestant Reformation and the closure of monasteries, scholars were encouraged to question ancient authority and think critically (Humanism). Instead of blindly copying Galen's ancient manuscripts, university medical faculties and commercial publishers actively sought and printed books detailing hands-on dissections and clinical experiments. Therefore, communication changed because the rise of Humanism created widespread demand for new scientific research rather than ancient Church-approved texts.",
