            type: 'written',
            tariff: 'Q5/Q6: Evaluative Essay [16+4 marks]',
            text: "'Approaches to treating and preventing disease during the Great Plague of 1665 showed almost complete continuity with the Black Death of 1348.' How far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16+4 marks]",
            stimulus: ['The Four Humours and miasma', 'Quarantine and watchmen'],
            model:
              "On the one hand, there is strong evidence to agree that approaches to treating and understanding disease during the Great Plague of 1665 showed almost complete continuity with the Black Death of 1348. In terms of medical theory, physicians in 1665 still had no idea that plague was caused by bacteria on rat fleas. As a result, the main explanation remained the ancient idea of miasma (bad air) alongside God’s punishment for sin. People carried sweet-smelling pomanders, smoked tobacco, and burned barrels of tar in the streets to clear the air, just as people had done in 1348. Furthermore, medical treatments were identical in failing to cure anyone: doctors still used traditional bloodletting and purging, strapped dried toads or plucked chickens to buboes, and sold useless 'Plague Water'. The slaughter of 200,000 cats and dogs—ordered because people thought animal fur caught the bad air—further proved medical ideas had not improved, as killing cats allowed the rat population to grow. Therefore, approaches to treating disease showed almost complete continuity with 1348 because physicians remained completely powerless without knowledge of germs.<br><br>On the other hand, approaches to preventing the disease showed clear change through organized action by local government using quarantine and watchmen. In 1348, government action was chaotic and councils did very little. In 1665, the Mayor of London enforced strict Plague Orders. Infected houses were padlocked shut for 28 days with a red cross painted on the door and guarded day and night by watchmen to prevent anyone from leaving or entering. Theatres and large public gatherings were banned, domestic animals were cleared, and mass plague pits were dug outside the city walls for night burials. Therefore, approaches to prevention showed significant change because local government enforced strict public health rules and systematic quarantine that did not exist during the Black Death.<br><br>Furthermore, approaches to investigating and recording the epidemic showed significant change through the use of scientific data collection. During the 1665 outbreak, London parishes appointed female 'Searchers of the Dead' to examine corpses, identify plague buboes, and report causes of death, which were published weekly in the official Bills of Mortality. This statistical record allowed the Lord Mayor and the newly founded Royal Society (1660) to track which parishes were most infected and observe when the epidemic was peaking, representing an early form of epidemiological tracking that was completely absent in 1348. In addition, leading physicians like Dr Nathaniel Hodges remained in London to take detailed clinical notes on the symptoms and conduct post-mortems, marking a shift towards empirical scientific observation. Therefore, approaches to recording disease showed major change because London developed systematic ways to track casualties and gather physical evidence that were never attempted during the Black Death.<br><br>In conclusion, I agree with the statement regarding medical treatment, but disagree regarding prevention and statistical tracking. In terms of medical cures and understanding causes, there was almost total continuity with 1348 because doctors were just as helpless without knowledge of germs. However, in terms of civic prevention and epidemiological tracking, 1665 showed clear progress through strict municipal quarantine, parish searchers, and weekly Bills of Mortality. Therefore, while medical science stayed the same, government organization and data collection improved significantly.",
