          model:
            'A student arguing that the NHS was the decisive turning point would focus on the profound transformation of healthcare access. Before 1948, healthcare was a commercial commodity; working-class families who could not afford private doctor fees or voluntary hospital subscriptions delayed treatment, leading to preventable deaths. Bevan’s NHS established that healthcare was a universal civil right free at the point of need. Conversely, a student arguing for alternative factors would demonstrate that state organization cannot cure disease without pharmacological breakthroughs. Paul Ehrlich’s Salvarsan 606 (1909), Domagk’s Prontosil (1932), and Florey and Chain’s penicillin (1940) created the actual cures that made NHS hospitals effective. Furthermore, the 1951 introduction of prescription charges proved that free care faced severe economic limits. The most sophisticated conclusion argues that while scientific magic bullets provided the medical tools, the NHS was the ultimate turning point because it ensured those life-saving tools reached every British citizen regardless of wealth.',
