          model:
            'A student arguing that Fleming was more important would emphasize that without his acute observational skill in September 1928, the mold would have been washed away in disinfectant. Fleming possessed the insight from his WWI battlefield service to recognize that the mold destroyed bacteria without harming human tissue, and his 1929 paper preserved the discovery for future scientists. Conversely, a student arguing for Florey and Chain would prove that Fleming’s discovery was medically useless for a decade because he failed to purify or stabilize the drug. Howard Florey, Ernst Chain, and Norman Heatley overcame immense biochemical challenges, freeze-drying the active substance, proving it cured infected mice in May 1940, and conducting the first human trial on Albert Alexander in 1941. Furthermore, Florey travelled to the USA in 1941 to orchestrate industrial deep-tank fermentation. The most persuasive historical judgement concludes that while Fleming provided the accidental spark, Florey and Chain’s team science and transatlantic industrialization were vastly more important, as they transformed an unstable laboratory curiosity into a mass-produced wonder drug that saved millions of lives.',
