            'A student arguing that government legislation was more important would emphasize that lung cancer remains notoriously difficult to cure, with five-year survival rates hovering around 16–20%. Therefore, prevention is far more effective than clinical treatment. Following Doll and Hill’s 1950 discovery, government systematically destroyed the market for cigarettes: banning television adverts in 1965, banning smoking in public places in 2007, and enforcing standardized plain packaging in 2016. This compulsory state action reduced adult smoking rates from 65% in 1950 to under 13% today, preventing hundreds of thousands of cancers from ever developing. Conversely, a student arguing for medical treatments would demonstrate that for individuals who already have tumors, legislation is useless; their lives depend on technological oncology. Low-dose CT scans, flexible bronchoscopes, robotic lobectomies, and modern immunotherapy drugs like pembrolizumab have given terminal patients extra years of life. The most sophisticated historical judgement concludes that while high-tech oncology represents the cutting edge of science, government legislation was vastly more important because it tackled the root environmental cause, achieving a profound statistical reduction in mortality that hospitals could never match.',
